cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software or its results, please cite it as below."
title: "Critical two-dimensional subtori of the torus: root systems, repeat lines, and torsion points"
abstract: >-
  A short note with Lean 4 verification on the maximal distance M from the coordinate walls of a two-dimensional
  subtorus of the n-torus given by integer linear forms in two variables. The coefficient lists of the positive
  roots of A2, B2, G2 have M = 1/3, 1/4, 1/6 (the tori U^2 of Jain and Kravitz and U_C of Cordella are the tori of
  B2 and G2; the upper bound is a finite case check closed by the Lean kernel). If n forms are pairwise distinct up
  to sign and fall into d >= 2 proportionality classes, at least 2n - d lines of the plane carry two coordinates
  that agree up to sign while no coordinate vanishes. A condition on the absolute values of the coordinates of an
  integer vector in the plane forces phi(N) = 2. If every form has content coprime to N and n is smaller than the
  number of points of the projective line over Z/N, then M >= 1/N; consequently primitive lists with n >= 5,
  n != 6 have M > 1/n, and M = 1/n for a primitive list forces n in {2,3,4,6}. One conjecture, supported by exact
  computation in bounded boxes, is stated and not proved. Novelty is partial: the values for B2, A2 and G2 are in
  the literature (Yi, Gill and Bu, arXiv:2610.40694; Cordella, arXiv:2609.03444); what is added is the Lean
  verification, the root-system dictionary, the sharp count 2n - d, and the torsion and totient bounds. The note
  has no bearing on the lonely runner conjecture itself. The Lean modules, the proofs and the first draft were
  produced by an AI system (Claude, Anthropic); the questions, directions and the decision to formalize these
  statements came from Vico Bonfioli. The note has not been peer reviewed.
authors:
  - family-names: Bonfioli
    given-names: Vico
